[snip]
> Some ideas might be granting all monsters in the group the same vision
> as the leader or even whole group (implying communication of some
> kind), lessen the chance of them fleeing, and maybe even increasing to-
> hit (assuming some communication about tactics from the leader) or to-
> damage (maybe assuming an increase in morale).  You could even go as
> far as subordinates trying to heal their leaders, or gaining the
> insight to not fire a wand or weapon when one of their group is in the
> crossfire.

Proposal: make hierarchical monsters always be generated with a leader. 
  Greatly increase the base cowardice of hierarchical monsters, so that 
attacking one will make it flee at half HP, or whatever, making them 
rather easier to defeat.  Then make them completely fearless whenever 
any leader is nearby and visible.  This will make the fear mechanic 
actually *do* something, which it doesn't much currently: too many 
monsters go straight from fearless to dead on the last hit.  Killing the 
leader will make a real difference to a battle (scattered monsters are 
much easier to defeat than concentrated ones).  Making the leader flee 
will also make them ineffective.  Then any way of causing fear becomes 
actually capable of tipping a battle in your favour.

Wizard and mage types should heal their companions, which would make 
then a prime target too.

As well as the obvious relationships, be sure to check the mythologies: 
E.g. vampires would act as leaders to any canines or bats, and the 
draconic Quest Nemeses would act as leaders to dragons.  An intriguing 
possibility is that young and/or eggs would act with the same mechanism 
of adults: They would fight to the death to defend them.  Similarly for 
other monsters that defend things.  (At least, I *think* the "defend and 
be en-couraged by" mechanism could be the same in both cases.)

